II degradation causes:
  - QKV/OUT/FFN1 II=3: W_qkv/W_out/W_ffn1 each have cyclic×64 dim=1 partition
    but the 256/384/1536 columns × K_UR=64 reads create memory port pressure.
    HLS schedules time-shared access → II=3 (= each j-iter takes 3 cycles).
  - FFN2 II=12: D_FFN=1536 outer dimension makes things worse. Per-j-output
    needs 64 W_ffn2 reads from a 1536-row × 384-column array. The cyclic×64
    on dim=1 gives 64 banks of 24 rows each — but 24 rows are spread across
    768 BRAM entries which can only serve 2 reads/cycle. 64 reads → 32 banks
    feeding 2 reads each → II=12 effectively.

